WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.847 --> 00:00:02.031
Our pump set again.

2
00:00:02.754 --> 00:00:07.282
To start, the motor has to go onto the grid, and that happens in the cabinet.

3
00:00:08.418 --> 00:00:09.823
In it sits a contactor,

4
00:00:09.986 --> 00:00:12.145
a heavy switch you operate electrically:

5
00:00:12.284 --> 00:00:15.674
current through its coil pulls three contacts shut at once,

6
00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:16.871
one per phase.

7
00:00:17.906 --> 00:00:21.749
That puts the motor straight onto the full grid: direct on line.

8
00:00:22.423 --> 00:00:27.543
Simple and cheap, but at the start it draws 6 to 8 times its rated current.

9
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:31.536
And the full torque gives a jolt on coupling and pump.

10
00:00:32.287 --> 00:00:36.826
With a big motor the voltage dips, and belts, couplings and gearboxes wear.

11
00:00:38.145 --> 00:00:39.074
Why that surge?

12
00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:41.860
At the start the rotor stands still,

13
00:00:42.035 --> 00:00:46.145
but the rotating field turns at synchronous speed straight away.

14
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:48.886
The slip is at its maximum:

15
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.843
the field cuts through the bars at full speed and induces big currents in them,

16
00:00:53.936 --> 00:00:56.084
which come from the grid through the stator.

17
00:00:56.838 --> 00:00:59.172
Almost a short-circuited transformer.

18
00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:03.687
As the rotor speeds up, the slip drops to a few percent,

19
00:01:03.872 --> 00:01:05.509
and the current with it to rated.

20
00:01:06.577 --> 00:01:10.768
With a heavy load that takes longer, and all that time the current stays high.

21
00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:14.734
Older and cheaper is the star-delta starter.

22
00:01:15.461 --> 00:01:17.887
The motor starts with its windings in star.

23
00:01:18.946 --> 00:01:23.927
That gives each winding less voltage: about a third of the direct-on-line current,

24
00:01:24.147 --> 00:01:25.819
but also a third of the torque.

25
00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:30.555
Near full speed it switches to delta, with the full voltage.

26
00:01:31.251 --> 00:01:34.386
The switch-over usually gives one more short surge.

27
00:01:35.364 --> 00:01:38.080
Good for motors that start light, like a fan.

28
00:01:38.786 --> 00:01:41.769
Under heavy load, a third of the torque is too little.

29
00:01:43.057 --> 00:01:48.119
A soft starter uses electronics to ramp up the voltage over a few seconds.

30
00:01:49.149 --> 00:01:53.653
That keeps the inrush current down, usually to 3 to 4 times rated,

31
00:01:53.816 --> 00:01:55.975
and the jolt becomes a gentle start.

32
00:01:57.061 --> 00:01:57.851
Up to speed,

33
00:01:58.037 --> 00:02:02.890
the motor simply runs at its fixed speed: a soft starter helps with starting,

34
00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:04.550
not with speed control.

35
00:02:05.581 --> 00:02:08.785
And less voltage gives much less torque: half the voltage,

36
00:02:08.937 --> 00:02:10.516
roughly a quarter of the torque.

37
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.171
A machine that starts under heavy load may stall.

38
00:02:15.472 --> 00:02:21.091
The variable frequency drive, the VFD or inverter, first turns the grid into DC,

39
00:02:21.393 --> 00:02:24.969
then uses fast switches to make a new three-phase supply,

40
00:02:25.143 --> 00:02:26.896
at a frequency of your choice.

41
00:02:27.895 --> 00:02:31.784
The rotating field follows that frequency: half the frequency,

42
00:02:31.924 --> 00:02:33.491
half the synchronous speed.

43
00:02:34.202 --> 00:02:36.408
That is how you control speed smoothly.

44
00:02:37.404 --> 00:02:41.468
When starting, it raises the frequency calmly to 50 hertz.

45
00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:46.083
The motor runs with little slip, and the current stays near rated.

46
00:02:47.128 --> 00:02:49.671
Because the voltage rises with the frequency,

47
00:02:49.845 --> 00:02:52.562
the motor still gives full torque from low speed.

48
00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:56.518
An example: a crusher for stone or scrap.

49
00:02:57.259 --> 00:03:03.378
Its 90 kilowatt motor draws about 160 amps rated, at 400 volts.

50
00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:06.032
Started direct on line,

51
00:03:06.206 --> 00:03:12.197
it draws roughly 1,000 to 1,300 amps for a few seconds at every start.

52
00:03:13.268 --> 00:03:15.671
The plant voltage dips: lights flicker,

53
00:03:15.857 --> 00:03:18.783
and two drives elsewhere trip on undervoltage.

54
00:03:19.797 --> 00:03:23.570
A soft starter cuts the surge to 3 to 4 times rated,

55
00:03:23.756 --> 00:03:26.310
but with that lower torque a full crusher may stall.

56
00:03:26.992 --> 00:03:32.135
The drive gives full torque at rated current: here the more expensive device fits.

57
00:03:33.422 --> 00:03:38.473
The gain is in pumps and fans: if the pump has to deliver less, you run it slower.

58
00:03:39.574 --> 00:03:43.602
By the affinity laws, flow goes with speed, pressure with its square,

59
00:03:43.846 --> 00:03:45.599
and power roughly with its cube.

60
00:03:46.686 --> 00:03:49.531
At 80 percent speed that is about half the power.

61
00:03:50.301 --> 00:03:52.333
In practice the saving is smaller.

62
00:03:53.343 --> 00:03:57.058
A common mistake: a drive left at 50 hertz all year.

63
00:03:57.765 --> 00:04:01.085
It saves nothing and only adds a few percent of losses.

64
00:04:02.412 --> 00:04:04.653
A drive also has side effects.

65
00:04:05.363 --> 00:04:07.244
It draws current in pulses,

66
00:04:07.476 --> 00:04:08.695
and that gives harmonics:

67
00:04:08.857 --> 00:04:13.072
currents at multiples of 50 hertz that distort the wave on the supply.

68
00:04:14.063 --> 00:04:16.014
The fast switching causes noise,

69
00:04:16.257 --> 00:04:20.924
and can build up a voltage on the motor shaft that discharges through the bearings.

70
00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:24.840
Each tiny spark through the grease pits the raceway,

71
00:04:25.096 --> 00:04:27.116
and the bearing fails much earlier.

72
00:04:28.142 --> 00:04:32.972
Shielded cables, filters and good earthing handle this, if they are designed in.

73
00:04:34.237 --> 00:04:37.163
The servo drive has an encoder on the motor shaft:

74
00:04:37.326 --> 00:04:41.029
a sensor that reports many times a second where the shaft is.

75
00:04:41.752 --> 00:04:45.026
That way it controls position and speed precisely.

76
00:04:46.014 --> 00:04:51.088
Think of robots, cut-to-length and packaging: it is about precision, not energy.

77
00:04:52.052 --> 00:04:54.676
So choosing starts with what the drive must do.

78
00:04:55.362 --> 00:04:58.950
Just starting: direct on line, or a soft starter.

79
00:04:59.684 --> 00:05:02.691
Varying speed: a variable frequency drive.

80
00:05:03.743 --> 00:05:06.959
And positioning to a tenth of a millimetre: a servo.
